data/orthodox/ — Orthodox texts and church history (docs/history/516), written by tools/orthodox-texts.py.
Every text here is in the public domain in the UK and the US: published before 1929 and its author or translator dead
more than seventy years. Nothing modern, nothing whose status was uncertain. Footnotes and editors' notes are left out.

From the Christian Classics Ethereal Library (ccel.org), which marks these texts public domain:
  Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, Second Series, ed. Schaff and Wace:
    vol. 1  (1890) Eusebius, Church History — A. C. McGiffert (d. 1933)
    vol. 2  (1890) Socrates, Ecclesiastical History — A. C. Zenos (d. 1942); Sozomen — C. D. Hartranft (d. 1914)
    vol. 3  (1892) Theodoret, Ecclesiastical History — Blomfield Jackson (d. 1913)
    vol. 4  (1892) Athanasius, Life of Antony — H. Ellershaw
    vol. 8  (1895) Basil, On the Holy Spirit — Blomfield Jackson (d. 1913)
    vol. 9  (1899) John of Damascus, An Exact Exposition of the Orthodox Faith — S. D. F. Salmond (d. 1905)
    vol. 14 (1900) The Seven Ecumenical Councils — H. R. Percival (d. 1903): canons, definitions and anathemas only
  Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, First Series, vol. 9 (1889): John Chrysostom, On the Priesthood, Instructions to
    Catechumens, Homilies on the Statues — W. R. W. Stephens (d. 1902), T. P. Brandram
  Ante-Nicene Fathers vol. 5 (1886): Cyprian, On the Unity of the Church — Ernest Wallis
  John Calvin, Institutes of the Christian Religion — Henry Beveridge (1845; d. 1863)
  Dionysius the Areopagite, The Mystical Theology — C. E. Rolt (1920; d. 1923). Two typing slips in CCEL's copy are
    put back to the printed words ("What is the Divine Gloom", "Trinity"); see FIX in the tool.
  Philip Schaff, History of the Christian Church, vols. 1–4 (1882–1910 edition; Schaff d. 1893)

From the Internet Archive:
  Isabel F. Hapgood, Service Book of the Holy Orthodox-Catholic Apostolic (Greco-Russian) Church (Boston, 1906;
    Hapgood d. 1928). The machine-read (OCR) text of Cornell University Library's copy,
    https://archive.org/details/cu31924029363128 — not proofread, and where the book prints the Liturgies of
    St John Chrysostom and St Basil side by side the two columns come one after the other.

Thomas Aquinas, Summa Theologiae (written 1265–1274), literally translated by Fathers of the English Dominican Province,
  second and revised edition (London: Burns Oates & Washbourne, 1920). Public domain: published before 1929, the
  translators long dead; also on Project Gutenberg (eBooks 17611, 17897, 18755, 19950). Text from the Christian
  Classics Ethereal Library, https://www.ccel.org/ccel/aquinas/summa — one card an article (Part.Question.Article),
  the Supplement and its two appendices included. Footnotes and editors' notes are left out.
